                            "description": "We will continue Romantic Poetry.Â  We completed Rime of the Ancient Mariner, students will be reading Kublia Khan with the rest of the romantics. Students will be reading Form and Meaning on page 830.Â  We will break into groups to deal with Byron, Shelley, and Keats, the last of the Romantics that we will focus on before we begin Frankenstein.Â  Students will break into groups to analyze and present the poems of these 4 poets.Â  Students will read and summarize biographical information for each poet; analyze the poem itself breaking down the parts; they will explain the poem and the poet's purpose as well as explain how it highlights the romantic movement; and then come up with a question for the class.Â  They will present their poems and analysis to the class.&#xA;***Before presentations, Read Shelley's &quot;A Defense of Poetry&quot; page 857Coleridge1.Kubla Khan page 830&#xA;Byron:Â &#xA;2.Â  She Walks in Beauty 834&#xA;3. When We Two Parted 836&#xA;4. Child Haroldâ€™s Pilgrimage 838-840&#xA;Shelley:&#xA;Â 5. Ozamandias 848&#xA;Â 6. Ode to the West Wind 850&#xA;Â 7. To A Skylark 853&#xA;Â *Read as a class A Defense of Poetry 857&#xA;Keats:Â &#xA;8. When I Have Fears That I May Cease To Be 862&#xA;9. To Autumn 864&#xA;10. Ode on a Grecian Urn 866&#xA;11. Ode to a Nightingale 868",
